# Cursor Rules for 3D Portfolio Project

## Project Overview

This is a modern 3D portfolio website built with React, Three.js (React Three Fiber), GSAP animations, and Tailwind CSS. The project showcases a personal portfolio with interactive 3D models, smooth animations, and responsive design.

## Tech Stack

- **Frontend**: React 19.2.0, Vite 7.1.12
- **3D Graphics**: Three.js 0.180.0, React Three Fiber 9.4.0 (@react-three/fiber), Drei 10.7.6 (@react-three/drei)
- **Animations**: GSAP 3.13.0 (@gsap/react 2.1.2)
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS v4.1.16
- **Email**: EmailJS 4.4.1 (@emailjs/browser)
- **Responsive**: react-responsive 10.0.1

## Code Style & Best Practices

### React Components

- Use functional components with hooks (React 19)
- Prefer named exports for components
- Keep components focused on a single responsibility
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks or utility functions
- Use JSX fragments (`<>...</>`) when no wrapper element is needed

### Three.js / React Three Fiber

- Always optimize 3D models (use transformed/optimized .glb files)
- Use `useFrame` for animations instead of requestAnimationFrame
- Implement proper cleanup for event listeners and animations
- Use Drei helpers when available (e.g., `OrbitControls`, `Html`, `useGLTF`)
- Cache 3D models using `useMemo` or preload them
- Keep canvas components separate from page components

### GSAP Animations

- Use `useGSAP` hook from `@gsap/react` for React components
- Always clean up animations and timelines
- Use refs to target DOM elements for animations
- Prefer `useGSAP` over `useEffect` + `gsap` when possible
- Create reusable animation utilities in a separate folder if needed

### Styling Guidelines

- Use Tailwind CSS utility classes (Tailwind v4)
- Follow mobile-first responsive design approach
- Use custom CSS only when Tailwind utilities aren't sufficient
- Keep responsive breakpoints consistent (md:, xl:, etc.)
- Use semantic class names when creating custom styles

### File Organization

```
src/
  components/        # Reusable UI components
    models/         # 3D model components (React Three Fiber)
  sections/         # Page sections (Hero, Experience, etc.)
  constants/        # Shared constants and configuration
```

### Component Structure

- Sections (`src/sections/`) are top-level page sections
- Components (`src/components/`) are reusable UI elements
- 3D models (`src/components/models/`) are React Three Fiber components
- Constants (`src/constants/`) contain shared data and configuration

### Naming Conventions

- Components: PascalCase (e.g., `Hero.jsx`, `AnimatedCounter.jsx`)
- Files: Match component name exactly
- Functions: camelCase
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE or camelCase for exported objects
- Props: camelCase

### Performance Optimization

- Use React.memo for components that don't need frequent re-renders
- Memoize expensive calculations with `useMemo`
- Use `useCallback` for functions passed as props to memoized components
- Lazy load 3D models where possible
- Optimize images and 3D models (use optimized/transformed versions)
- Implement loading states for 3D scenes

### Error Handling

- Always handle loading errors for 3D models gracefully
- Provide fallback UI for failed image/model loads
- Use try-catch blocks for async operations (like EmailJS)
- Show user-friendly error messages

### Code Quality

- Remove unused imports and variables
- Keep functions small and focused
- Add comments for complex 3D math or animation logic
- Use meaningful variable names
- Avoid deeply nested conditionals

### Environment Variables

- Store EmailJS credentials in `.env` file
- Use `VITE_APP_` prefix for Vite environment variables
- Never commit `.env` file to version control
- Document required environment variables in README

### Git Workflow

- Use Gitmoji for commit messages
- Make atomic commits (one feature/fix per commit)
- Write clear, descriptive commit messages
- Never commit large binary files (models, images) directly - use CDN or optimize

### Testing Considerations

- Test animations on different devices and browsers
- Verify 3D scenes work on lower-end devices
- Test responsive breakpoints thoroughly
- Validate EmailJS integration works correctly

### Accessibility

- Add alt text to all images
- Ensure keyboard navigation works for interactive elements
- Maintain proper color contrast
- Add ARIA labels where appropriate for 3D scenes

### Build & Deployment

- Use Vite build commands (`npm run build`)
- Optimize assets before deploying
- Check bundle size and optimize if needed
- Test production build locally before deploying

## Specific Patterns for This Project

### GSAP Animations in Sections

```jsx
useGSAP(() => {
  gsap.fromTo(
    ".hero-text h1",
    { y: 50, opacity: 0 },
    { y: 0, opacity: 1, stagger: 0.2, duration: 1, ease: "power2.inOut" }
  );
});
```

### 3D Model Components

- Create separate component files for each 3D scene
- Use `useGLTF` from Drei to load models
- Implement camera controls and lighting appropriately
- Handle responsive behavior for 3D scenes

### Constants Management

- Keep all static data in `src/constants/index.js`
- Export named exports for better tree-shaking
- Group related constants together (e.g., `expCards`, `testimonials`)

### Section Components

- Each section should be self-contained
- Use semantic HTML5 elements (`<section>`, `<header>`, etc.)
- Include proper IDs for anchor links in navigation
- Keep section logic minimal - extract to components when needed

## What to Avoid

- Don't import entire libraries when you only need specific functions
- Don't create inline styles when Tailwind classes work
- Don't mutate props directly
- Don't forget to clean up GSAP animations and Three.js resources
- Don't hardcode configuration values - use constants or env variables
- Don't commit large binary assets to git
- Don't skip responsive design considerations

## Code Review Checklist

- [ ] Components are properly exported
- [ ] No console.log statements in production code
- [ ] All images have alt text
- [ ] 3D models are optimized versions
- [ ] Animations have proper cleanup
- [ ] Responsive design tested on multiple screen sizes
- [ ] Environment variables are properly configured
- [ ] No unused imports or dead code
